# 5.0.0

`useStructureRestApi` is rebuilt directly on [TanStack Query](https://tanstack.com/query) as the
single source of truth for records, lists, searches, freshness, invalidation, and loading. TanStack
came in with 2.0 (`afb162b`) only to replace the old TTL timestamps: the hand-rolled dictionary
stayed around it through 4.x, so the toolkit ran **two** caches, and TanStack was used as a clock
that never knew what was actually on screen. This release closes four related frontend bugs traced
to that split:
- the next user to log in could still see the previous user's data (nothing scoped the cache to
  *who* the data belonged to);
- a language switch emptied whatever page was on screen (same root cause: nothing kept fetched
  pages tied to a language);
- a paginated list's pager could vanish on a cache hit (the server-reported total was never stored
  alongside the page that produced it);
- another store's list could go stale on screen indefinitely (invalidating it did nothing, because
  nothing was watching it live).

All four are now structural, not patched: a resource's cache lives entirely in one shared
`QueryClient`, scoped by a `dependsOn` getter (whose data, which language), and every `watch*` call
is an active TanStack observer that reacts to invalidation on its own.

## Changed

- **BREAKING** — `@guebbit/vue-toolkit` now peers on `@tanstack/vue-query ^5.103` instead of
  `@tanstack/query-core`. `vue-query` **is** `query-core` plus the Vue layer (it re-exports all of
  it and depends on an exact version internally), so installing `@tanstack/query-core` directly
  alongside it is a real failure, not a warning — two copies means a client built from the wrong
  one silently never fetches, and `instanceof CancelledError` answers `false` across the pair.
  Remove `@tanstack/query-core` from your own `package.json`, install `@tanstack/vue-query`, and
  set it up once per app:
  ```ts
  import { QueryClient, VueQueryPlugin } from '@tanstack/vue-query'
  const queryClient = new QueryClient()
  app.use(VueQueryPlugin, { queryClient })
  ```
  4.x's private client set `retry: false` and `networkMode: 'always'`; a `new QueryClient()`
  keeps TanStack's defaults instead, so a `watch*` query retries a failure 3 times (in a browser)
  before reporting it, and queries and mutations pause while the browser reports itself offline.
  To keep the 4.x behaviour, set them on the client:
  ```ts
  new QueryClient({
      defaultOptions: {
          queries: { retry: false, networkMode: 'always' },
          mutations: { networkMode: 'always' }
      }
  })
  ```
  There is no private, toolkit-owned `QueryClient` any more — every `useStructureRestApi`/
  `useStructureSearchApi`/`useStructureCrudApi` call either takes one explicitly via its
  `queryClient` option or finds it through this injection (which also works inside a Pinia setup
  store). One shared client per app is what makes cross-resource cache invalidation possible at
  all. `vue` peer moves to `>=3.4` — one minor past `vue-query`'s own floor (`3.3`): see Fixed,
  below, for why.
- **BREAKING** — `loadingKey` → **`resourceKey`**, and it is now **required**, with no random
  fallback. It's the key every query and mutation a resource makes starts with — what
  `queryClient.invalidateQueries({queryKey:[resourceKey]})` addresses from another store, and what
  `setQueryDefaults` uses to keep records cached. A random default would silently defeat both for
  any resource that forgot to set one. The returned `loadingKey` field is renamed the same way:
  read `resourceKey`.
- **BREAKING** — `TTL` → **`staleTime`**, both as a composable option and a per-call setting. "Time
  to live" implied the data gets removed once it expires; it doesn't — stale data keeps rendering
  while a fresh copy downloads, same as always. Only the name changed.
- **BREAKING** — `lastUpdateKey: 'x'` → **`key: ['x']`**, now an array. It still namespaces
  independent buckets for an otherwise-identical list or `fetchAny` call, and is now also what
  `isLoading(key)` matches a call by — on queries **and** mutations (`createTarget`,
  `updateTarget`, `deleteTarget`, `mutateAny` take it for that). `fetchTarget`/`fetchMultiple`/
  `watchTarget` take no `key`: a record has exactly one cache entry, keyed only by id.
  `checkAny`'s first argument follows: `checkAny('x')` → `checkAny(['x'])`.
- **BREAKING** — per-call settings are typed per method: each method accepts only the fields it
  reads, and passing another is a type error. `fetchTarget`/`fetchMultiple`: `forced`, `merge`,
  `staleTime`. `fetchAny`: `forced`, `staleTime`, `key`. `createTarget`/`deleteTarget`/`mutateAny`:
  `key`. `updateTarget`: `merge`, `key`, `applyResponse` (`IUpdateTargetSettings`). `watchTarget`:
  `forced`, `merge`, `staleTime` plus its callbacks (`IWatchTargetSettings`). `checkAll`/
  `checkByParent`/`checkPaginate`: `key`, `staleTime`; `checkTarget`/`checkMultiple`/`checkAny`:
  `staleTime` (`checkAny`'s key is its first argument). List fetches and their watchers take the
  whole `IFetchSettings`.
- **BREAKING** — `mismatch` → **`partial`**, same dual effect (merge instead of replace, and don't
  count the write as freshly-fetched) — renamed because "the response holds partial records" is
  what it always meant; "mismatch" described nothing about the actual behaviour.
- **BREAKING** — `fetchAgain` → **`applyResponse`** (`updateTarget` only): trust the response as the
  record's new, full data. Same option, clearer name — it never re-fetched anything, the old name
  promised a request that doesn't happen. An empty response (`undefined`/`null`) keeps the
  optimistic patch as the record; the resource's lists are invalidated either way.
- **BREAKING** — records are read-only. 4.x's `itemDictionary` and `parentHasMany` were writable
  refs, so writing into a record read back (`getRecord(1).name = 'x'`) or into the dictionary
  changed the store. On `useStructureRestApi` they are now computed views over the cache: a write
  into `getRecord(x)` or `itemDictionary` is ignored with a Vue warning, and `parentHasMany` is
  rebuilt from the cache on every change, so an in-place edit of it is lost. Migration: write
  through `addRecord`/`editRecord`/`updateTarget`, and link children with `addToParent`.
- **BREAKING** — `IToastType` → **`EToastType`** (`useNotificationsStore`). The `I` prefix names an
  interface; an enum gets no such prefix. Bundled into this unreleased major rather than left for
  the next one. Update the import and every reference.
- **BREAKING** — `fetchMultiple` resolves the requested ids: one slot per id, the fetched ones
  first, then the cached ones, `undefined` for an id the server left out. 4.x resolved every item
  the server returned (whatever its id), then the cached ones.
- **BREAKING** — raw cache keys carry record and parent ids as strings:
  `[resourceKey, 'target', dependsOn, String(id)]`,
  `[resourceKey, 'parent', dependsOn, String(parentId), ...key]`. `5` and `'5'` (a route param)
  now address one entry. A raw `queryClient` call that addresses a record or a parent list must
  use the string id.
- **BREAKING** (types) — `useStructureCrudApi`'s read operations take no `options` parameter:
  `list: () => …`, `search: (filters, page, pageSize) => …`, `get: (id) => …`. It was never
  forwarded to them; `O` now types only `create`/`update`/`remove`. `updateTarget`'s `apiCall` is
  typed `() => Promise<F>` (4.x: `() => Promise<F | (T | undefined)[]>`); a response that is not
  a record object (empty, an array, a primitive) keeps the optimistic patch.
- **BREAKING** — `forced` keeps its name and its intent (skip the cache, get fresh data), but its
  mechanism changed: it now runs the call with `staleTime: 0` instead of dropping the cache entry
  first. A concurrent, non-forced call for the same thing joins this same request instead of racing
  it — dropping the entry first would have cancelled that other call with a rejection.
- **BREAKING** — a search's `apiCall` (and `fetchSearch`/`watchSearch`/the CRUD `search` operation)
  now resolves **`{ items, totalItems }`** instead of a bare array, and so do `fetchSearch`, the
  watcher's `search()`, `searchNow()` and `resetFilters()`. `totalItems` is stored alongside the
  page it came from, so it — and the derived `pageTotal` — now survive a cache hit, which closes
  the vanishing-pager bug above. `fetchPage` (unfiltered, via `fetchPaginate`) still resolves a bare
  array and discards `totalItems`.
- **BREAKING** — **The applied search.** What `pageItemList`, `pageTotal` and `totalItems` show
  follows the *applied* search — a detached copy of the filters (plus its `key`) taken when a
  search is applied — not the live filters. Only `search()`, `fetchSearch`, and the CRUD
  `searchNow()`/`resetFilters()` apply the live filters (and `watchSearch` once at creation, with
  `immediate`); a page or page-size change fetches another page of the applied search without
  re-reading them. Fixes a real bug: binding a filter form's inputs straight to the live `filters`
  blanked the list while the user typed, before Search was pressed. Migration: call `search()` (or
  `searchNow()`) wherever an edit of the filters should take effect.
- **BREAKING** — on `useStructureSearchApi`/`useStructureCrudApi`, changing `pageSize` now also
  resets `pageCurrent` to `1`, in the same fetch — a page number from the old page size rarely
  means anything under the new one. The client-side pagination of `useStructureDataManagement`/
  `useStructureRestApi` is unchanged.
- **BREAKING** — every watcher returns a handle **`{ stop, refetch, error }`** (`IWatchHandle`).
  `watchTarget` (and the CRUD `watchOne`) returned a bare stop function (`WatchStopHandle`), and
  `watchSearch` (the CRUD `watchList`) returned `{ stop, search }`. Migration:
  `const stop = watchTarget(...)` → `const { stop } = watchTarget(...)`. `refetch()` fetches now,
  joins a fetch already running instead of restarting it, and resolves with the watched data as
  cached after the fetch (a failure leaves the previous data); a watcher never rejects — failures
  show in `error` (and `onError` where offered). `watchSearch` adds `search()`
  (`IWatchSearchHandle`), which resolves `undefined` on failure; `watchAny` adds `data`.
- **BREAKING** — `useStructureCrudApi`'s `operations` parameter is required: it no longer defaults
  to `{}`. `settings` was already effectively required, since it carries `resourceKey`. Migration:
  `useStructureCrudApi()` → `useStructureCrudApi({}, { resourceKey: '...' })`.
- **BREAKING** — a resource's data outlives the effect scope that built it. 4.x cleared the
  dictionary (and its private client) when the owning component or store was disposed; now
  stopping a scope ends the resource's cache subscriptions and its watchers, and every entry stays
  in the shared `QueryClient`. Call `resetAll()` first, or let a `dependsOn` change drop it, when
  data must leave memory.
- `watchTarget`/`watchSearch` (and the CRUD `watchOne`/`watchList`) are now **active**: built on
  TanStack's `useQuery`, so an `invalidateQueries` call from anywhere, or a `dependsOn` change,
  re-runs them on their own — no imperative refetch needed.
- `watchOne(idSource, settings?)` forwards `settings` (`forced`, `merge`, `staleTime`, and the
  `onSuccess`/`onError`/`onSettled` callbacks) to `watchTarget`.
- `loading` stays on every resource, now derived from TanStack: true while any query or mutation
  under the resource's `resourceKey` is in flight (the same as `isLoading()`).
- `resetAll()` now drops every kind of a resource's entries under the current `dependsOn`
  (records, lists, searches) in one call, rather than only resetting the local dictionary and
  parent relations, and refetches what active watchers show: the views empty, then the watched
  data comes back. `resetRecords()` removes records only, and marks the lists stale (their ids may
  now point at nothing), so active list watchers refetch.
- Cache lifetime: records and parent lists (`getListByParent`) are never garbage-collected, as the
  4.x dictionary never was. The `all`/`page`/`search`/`any` entries follow the client's `gcTime`
  (TanStack's default: 5 minutes in a browser once nothing observes them) — 4.x kept its own
  search page index, and gave its private client a `gcTime` of at least `TTL`. A search page
  fetched imperatively with `fetchSearch` therefore empties `pageItemList`/`totalItems` 5 minutes
  after nothing observes it; `watchSearch` keeps it alive.
- `maxRecords`' default drops from `100_000` to `10_000` — a record costs more as a TanStack query
  than as a plain object (measured for 10 000 records: ~2.2 MB as plain objects, ~10.5 MB as
  queries, i.e. ~0.8–1 KB more per record), so the same backstop now sits at a tighter bound.
- Crossing `maxRecords` now drops every other entry of the current scope — lists and searches too,
  not only records — while the list fetch that crossed it keeps its own entry, resolving its items
  and caching its list. Only records not cached yet count towards the bound (4.x counted the whole
  incoming batch), so refetching a list that is already cached never triggers the wipe.
  Queries still fetching are spared, since their answers are on the way. `fetchMultiple` enforces
  the bound as well; `fetchTarget`/`watchTarget` no longer trigger it (in 4.x they did), so no
  single-record write does: the mutations never did.
- `parentHasMany`/`getRecordsByParent`/`getListByParent` on `useStructureRestApi` read the cache: a
  parent's children are the union of its keyless entry and every `fetchByParent(..., { key })`
  bucket for it, in first-seen order, without duplicates. `addToParent` writes the keyless entry;
  `removeFromParent`/`removeDuplicateChildren` edit every bucket of the parent. Edited entries are
  marked stale, so the next `fetchByParent` asks the server.

## Added

- **`dependsOn`** — a getter returning the values a resource's data depends on (whose data, which
  language): `dependsOn: () => [session.viewer?.id ?? 'guest', locale.value]`. Read fresh at the
  start of every call; when it changes, the resource's entries under the old value — records
  included — are cancelled and removed (not merely marked stale), every active `watch*` re-runs
  under the new one, and an answer or rollback that settles afterwards is dropped. Closes the
  cross-user and cross-language data leaks above.
- **`watchAll`**, **`watchByParent`**, **`watchAny`** — the active counterparts of `fetchAll`/
  `fetchByParent`/`fetchAny`, so a view never has to fetch imperatively from `onMounted` or a click
  handler. `watchAll`/`watchByParent` take the full `IFetchSettings` (`forced`: every mount or key
  switch asks the server); `watchByParent`'s `parentId` may be a plain value, a Ref or a getter,
  and re-runs when it changes, and its `apiCall` receives the parent id the running query is for. `watchAny(apiCall, { key, forced?, staleTime? })` requires `key` (an
  active query needs a stable identity) and also returns `data`, since its answer isn't a record.
- **`mutateAny(apiCall, { key? })`** — a command that doesn't fit `createTarget`/`updateTarget`/
  `deleteTarget`'s record shape, run as a one-shot TanStack mutation. Does not auto-invalidate
  anything — it has no defined relationship to any record or list.
- **`isLoading(key?)`** on every resource — a plain function, true while a query or mutation of
  the resource runs whose `key` starts with the given segments (`isLoading(['dash'])` covers a call
  keyed `['dash', 'w1']`); no key: anything of the resource. Call it inside a `computed`.
- **`useIsLoading(prefixes?, queryClient?)`** — a new standalone composable: "is any of these
  resources busy" across the whole app, matching by `resourceKey` string prefix (the same rule
  `useCoreStore`'s `isLoading` already uses). Built on `useIsFetching`/`useIsMutating`; the optional
  `queryClient` is for code with no injection context.
- **`totalItems`** on `useStructureSearchApi`/`useStructureCrudApi` — the applied search's
  server-reported total, for "124 orders"; `pageTotal` is now derived from it instead of a local
  item count. While a new page loads it keeps the applied search's latest known total.
- `searchGet(filters, page, pageSize, { key? })` — an optional fourth argument reads a page cached
  under a `key` bucket.
- `resourceKey` and `editRecords` on the object `useStructureRestApi` returns.
- Exported types: `IWatchHandle`, `IWatchCallbacks`, `IWatchTargetSettings`, `IWatchSearchHandle`,
  `ISearchResult`, `IUpdateTargetSettings`, `IRecordStore`.
- `useStructureDataManagement` gains an optional, advanced third parameter, `recordStore`: the
  write surface its CRUD methods go through instead of owning a plain `ref` directly. Defaults to a
  local, in-memory store (so the composable is unaffected on its own) — `useStructureRestApi`
  supplies a TanStack-backed one, which is what turns `itemDictionary` into a live, read-only view
  of the query cache rather than a second copy of it.
- Documentation page for `useIsLoading`, and diagrams of the cache layout, the `dependsOn` flow and
  the applied search.

## Removed

- **BREAKING** — `destroy()`, `startLoading()`/`stopLoading()`, `saveRecords`, the `getLoading`/
  `setLoading` options, the per-call `loading` and `loadingKey` settings, `fetchLike`,
  `searchCleanup()`/`resetSearches()`/`searchCached` and the `ISearchCache` type.
  - `destroy()`: there is no private client left to tear down; stopping the owning scope ends the
    subscriptions and watchers, and `resetAll()` removes the data.
  - Loading: `loading` itself stays (see Changed); what is gone is the manual ref-count and its
    wiring. TanStack already knows what is in flight, so there is nothing to connect an external
    store to (`getLoading`/`setLoading`), and no per-call opt-out (`loading: false`) or per-call
    loading name (`loadingKey`): tag a call with `key` and ask `isLoading(key)`.
  - `fetchLike`: storing the record "as fetched" is now what `createTarget` always does, and what
    `updateTarget` does with its response; `updateTarget` opts out with `applyResponse: false`.
  - The search page→ids index: a search's pages are cache entries now, read directly; there is no
    separate index to clean up or cap.

## Fixed

- A resource's start-up sweep dropped every cache entry of its `resourceKey` under any other
  `dependsOn` value, and a `dependsOn` switch dropped everything under the old value the same way —
  both on the assumption that nothing still alive could be using it. True for an ordinary scope
  change, false the moment a second instance of the same `resourceKey` is alive under a different
  scope: creating it wiped the first instance's data, and either instance moving its `dependsOn`
  away from a scope the other was still showing dropped that scope's data out from under it. A new
  live-scope registry (`src/internal/scopeRegistry.ts`) tracks how many instances currently claim
  each scope; the sweep and the switch's drop now only remove a scope nothing claims any more, so
  two instances of the same resource under different scopes coexist.
- `fetchTarget`/`watchTarget` by an alternate key stored the record twice: `fetchTarget(apiCall,
  'my-slug')` resolving `{ id: 7 }` wrote the record under `7` (its own id) and, separately, under
  `'my-slug'` (whatever TanStack writes the query function's return value back under) — two
  independent copies that would silently diverge the moment either one changed. The requested key
  now holds an alias entry instead (pointing at `7`, never a copy of the record); `getRecord`,
  `selectedRecord` and everything that reads through `getRecord` follow it one hop, and
  `itemDictionary`/`itemList` only ever see the record once.
- `watchTarget` cleared `selectedIdentifier` on any failed fetch, including a background refetch of
  a record already on screen: a transient network error blanked the detail view even though the
  previously fetched record was still sitting right there in the cache. The selection is now
  cleared only when it still points at the failed id **and** nothing is cached for it any more —
  a genuine "there is nothing left to show" case, not "the latest refetch happened to fail".
- A watcher's `onSuccess`/`onError`/`onSettled` ran synchronously inside TanStack's own query-cache
  notify dispatch. A callback that threw there corrupted that dispatch mid-flight — TanStack read
  it as the fetch itself failing, putting the query into an error state, firing `onError` for a
  fetch that had actually succeeded, and (on `watchTarget`) clearing the selection. The callbacks
  now run a microtask after the dispatch that triggered them has fully finished, so a throw inside
  one surfaces as an ordinary uncaught error instead.
- TanStack's `hydrate()` — what `persistQueryClient` uses to restore a cache persisted on a
  previous visit — added a restored query straight into the cache already holding data, without
  ever going through `.setData()`, so only an `added` cache event fired, never `updated`/`success`.
  The record view (`itemDictionary`, `itemList`, `getRecord`, …) is a computed keyed off a data
  counter that only moves on a counted cache event; an uncounted `added` left it stuck on its old
  (usually empty) value until something unrelated to the restore changed the same kind. An `added`
  event with data now counts too.
- `updateTarget`/`deleteTarget` snapshotted the record to roll back to **before** cancelling its
  in-flight read, not right before applying the optimistic edit: two mutations on the same id
  started in the same tick both captured the same "before either ran" snapshot. If the older one
  succeeded and the newer one then failed, the newer one's rollback restored that shared snapshot —
  undoing the older one's already-confirmed change, not just its own — and left the record stamped
  as fresh with no way to tell it was wrong. The snapshot is now taken right before applying, so a
  same-tick sibling's rollback returns to what the other call itself left behind; and a rollback, or
  a success skipped because a newer mutation now owns the record, invalidates it, so an active
  watcher reconciles it with the server instead of trusting the local guess.
- `updateTarget`/`deleteTarget` cancelled every list read of the resource's current scope (`all`,
  `parent`, `page`, `search`), not just the record's own: an unrelated in-flight `fetchAll` (or a
  search page, or another parent's list) resolved `[]` on that cancellation and never retried on
  its own. Reads were also unprotected the other way — `fetchTarget`'s id-less path could resurrect
  a record a `deleteTarget` had just removed, and `fetchMultiple` could overwrite a value an
  `updateTarget` had just applied, whichever answer happened to arrive last. A mutation now cancels
  only the record's own query; a per-resource write guard tracks which id a mutation owns and for
  how long, and every read's write to a record — `fetch*`/`watch*` and search alike — is dropped
  once a mutation on that id has started since the read began, or is still running.
- `useStructureDataManagement`/`useStructureRestApi`'s composite identifiers (`identifiers: ['a',
  'b']`) collided whenever a value contained the delimiter: `{ a: 'x|y', b: 'z' }` and
  `{ a: 'x', b: 'y|z' }` both joined to `'x|y|z'`, so adding the second silently overwrote the
  first. `createIdentifier`, `getRecord`'s multi-argument form and `editRecord`'s array `id` now
  escape the delimiter (and a literal backslash) inside each value before joining, so different
  tuples never produce the same id. An id whose values contain neither character is unchanged.
- `updateTarget`'s success handler applied the server response unconditionally once its scope was
  still current, with no check for a newer mutation on the same id: a `deleteTarget` removing a
  record while an older `updateTarget` for it was still in flight could be undone by that update's
  late, stale success — resurrecting a record the app had just deleted. Success now applies only
  while the record still holds exactly what this call's own optimistic edit left it as, the same
  rule the rollback path already followed for a failure.
- `createTarget` left the `dummyData` placeholder on screen when the API resolved empty; it is now
  removed whatever the call resolved, and the lists are marked stale on any success.
- A failed `fetchOne` cleared the selection even when a newer `fetchOne` had selected another id
  meanwhile; it now clears it only while it is still the failed id.
- `useStructureCrudApi`'s `resetFilters()` restored whatever had been typed instead of the initial
  filters: `ref(initialFilters)` wrapped the caller's object, so a form editing `filters` in place
  rewrote `initialFilters` too. `filters` is now a detached copy that never shares objects with
  `initialFilters`.
- `removeFromParent` on a parent with no recorded children threw a `TypeError`; it now leaves that
  parent with an empty list.
- `useStructureDataManagement`'s `getRecordsByParent`/`getListByParent` returned nothing for a
  parent id of `0` or `''`; those are real parent ids now, and only an omitted (`undefined`) one
  means "no parent".
- `docs/guide/getting-started.md` claimed `@tanstack/query-core`/`vue-query` were regular
  dependencies landing in your bundle automatically; neither was ever true (the former was a peer,
  the latter wasn't referenced at all). Corrected alongside the peer-dependency change above.
- `dist`'s `.js.map`/`.d.ts.map` files pointed at `src/`, which the package never ships; a
  debugger stepping into the library, or a bundler resolving a source map, hit a 404. `sourceMap`
  and `declarationMap` are off; `dist`'s `.d.ts` files still carry full types, just no map.
- Under Vue 3.3, changing `pageSize` on `useStructureSearchApi`/`useStructureCrudApi` could refetch
  the *old* page number at the *new* page size before resetting to page 1, firing one extra
  request: Vue 3.3's watcher scheduler could flush the active query's key before the
  `pageSize`→`pageCurrent` reset watcher runs. `vue` peer floor moves to `>=3.4`, which orders them
  correctly.
- `dist` couldn't be loaded by plain Node or by `moduleResolution: node16`/`nodenext`:
  `tsc` emitted extensionless relative imports (`'./stores/core'`), which Node's own ESM loader
  refuses (`ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`). A bundler-free consumer — SSR that externalizes the package,
  some test runners — hit this on every import. `src/` now imports its own modules with a `.js`
  extension, and `tsconfig.json` builds with `module`/`moduleResolution: NodeNext`, so the compiler
  refuses a missing one from now on.

# 4.0.0

## Changed
- **BREAKING** — `useCoreStore`'s `isLoading` is a function taking optional key prefixes, not a computed. `isLoading()` still answers "is anything loading at all"; `isLoading(['account', 'cart'])` answers only for the keys starting with one of those. The old computed collapsed an already-granular dictionary back to one bit before any UI saw it, so a background poll lit the same indicator as a user's save. Replace `core.isLoading` with `core.isLoading()`, and note it no longer comes out of `storeToRefs` — destructure it from the store and call it inside a `computed`.
- `loadings` is typed `Record<string, boolean>`: `Object.entries` never returned the `symbol` half, so a symbol key could be set and then never matched by anything reading the dictionary.

# 3.1.0

## Added
- Added `useAsyncAction`: loading/data/error state for ONE async call, and the wrapper that drives it. The counterpart to the `useStructure*` family rather than a competitor — those are about records (identified, cached, mutated), this is about a one-shot call whose answer is just a payload. `fetchAny` already covered the loading half but exposes neither `data` nor `error`, which is what left a dashboard writing the same three-ref block once per endpoint. It never rejects: a failure lands in `error` and the promise still resolves, so one dead endpoint renders one error panel instead of blanking the page. That makes it a composable for READS — a write whose outcome the user asked for should reject and let the view answer with the toast it already writes. Only the newest `run` may write, so a double-clicked Refresh cannot overwrite a fresh answer with a stale one, nor clear `loading` while its successor is still in flight.
- Added `useLivenessProbe`: a connectivity banner's whole state in one boolean. Probes once on creation, again on every browser `online` event, then retries slowly and ONLY while down — a reachable target is never polled twice. The retry chain has exactly one owner, which is the failure this exists to prevent: two `online` events while down otherwise leave two independent loops running, each spawning its own successor forever, with teardown able to cancel only the last one scheduled. What is being probed is the caller's business — anything that rejects when unreachable will do. Stops with the surrounding effect scope; `stop()` is idempotent and a probe that outlives teardown can no longer write to `down`.
- Exported `IAsyncAction`, `IAsyncActionSettings`, `TErrorResolver`, `ILivenessProbe` and `ILivenessProbeSettings`.
- Added documentation pages for `useAsyncAction` and `useLivenessProbe`.

## Changed
- **BREAKING for anyone pinning it** — `@guebbit/js-toolkit` moved from `^1.2.1` to `^2.2.0`. `useAsyncAction`'s default error resolver is its new `extractErrorMessage`, which is what makes a plain-object rejection work: an interceptor that normalises failures rejects with an object literal rather than an `Error`, and anything testing `instanceof Error` reads a real API refusal as "nothing usable". Nothing else in the package changed behaviour.

# 3.0.0

## Removed
- **BREAKING** — removed `dialogs` from `useNotificationsStore`. It was a bare `Record<string, boolean>` with no actions of its own, so it carried no behaviour worth sharing: dialog visibility is an application concern and belongs in a consumer's own store. Replace `notifications.dialogs[name]` with a local `ref({} as Record<string, boolean>)`.

## Added
- Added `useStructureCrudApi`, the highest-level entry point: a whole resource — list, filtered search, read, create, update, delete — declared as the API calls that reach it. It is `useStructureSearchApi` with the wiring done, and spreads everything that composable returns, so it is a convenience and never a ceiling. Every operation (`list`, `search`, `get`, `create`, `update`, `remove`) is optional; a method whose operation was not supplied returns a rejection naming it. Owns the `filters` ref, so the filters driving the search and the filters scoping `pageItemList` cannot be two different objects. `optimisticPatch` covers the case where the update payload is not a partial record — a multipart form whose payload carries a `File` the record has no business holding.
- Added `useUploadProgress`: reactive `progress`/`isUploading` for one upload, plus a `track` wrapper that returns to idle however the request ends. HTTP-client-agnostic — the client-specific part is a single `buildOptions` function passed in, so the toolkit never names axios. `track(send, { enabled: false })` calls `send` with no options at all, for the form whose file is optional.
- `useStructureFormValidation` now owns `showFormErrors` across the whole submit flow: `handleSubmit` reveals on a validation failure, hides on success, and leaves it off when the handler itself throws (an API failure is not a statement about any field).
- Added `revealErrors()` to `useStructureFormValidation`: turns `showFormErrors` on, waits for the render, focuses the first invalid field, then calls `onInvalid`. The wait is why it is a method — fields only acquire their invalid markers once the flag has propagated, so focusing any earlier finds nothing.
- Added `applyServerErrors(error, options?)` to `useStructureFormValidation`: attaches the errors an API rejected a submit with to the fields they belong to. Finds them at the root, under `.data` or under `.response.data`, and understands field maps, `{ field, message }` lists (including `name`/`param`/`path` and `msg` spellings) and bare string lists. Merges rather than replaces, and returns `false` when the rejection carried nothing displayable, so the caller knows it still owes the user a message.
- Added `formElement`, `invalidFieldSelector` and `onInvalid` options to `useStructureFormValidation`. Focus targets `[aria-invalid="true"]` by default; omitting `formElement` makes `revealErrors` a pure state change with no DOM access, for SSR and node tests.
- Exported `IStructureFormValidation`, `IStructureCrudApi` and `IUploadProgress` return-type aliases, plus `DEFAULT_INVALID_FIELD_SELECTOR`.

## Changed
- Documented that `watchTarget` selects the record it fetches while `fetchTarget` does not, and why: `watchTarget` is bound to a screen's id, whereas a prefetch or background refresh through `fetchTarget` must not steal the selection from what the user is looking at. `useStructureCrudApi`'s `fetchOne` is the selecting variant.
- Added documentation pages for `useStructureSearchApi` (previously undocumented despite being a core composable), `useStructureCrudApi` and `useUploadProgress`.
- `handleSubmit` is written as a promise chain rather than `async`/`await` with `try`/`finally`, matching the style of the rest of the package. Behaviour is unchanged: the `Promise.resolve()` the chain starts from is what keeps a *synchronous* throw inside the submit handler arriving at the caller as a rejection, exactly as `await` did. `src/` no longer contains `async`/`await` anywhere.

# 2.2.0

## Added
- `useStructureFormValidation` takes a third `options` argument with a `revalidateOn` option: a `WatchSource` (or array of them) that re-runs `validate()` over the unchanged form data whenever it changes. It exists for language switches — `formErrors` holds resolved strings, so an error already on screen cannot re-translate itself no matter how the schema is shaped, and only re-parsing produces the new wording. It fires only while errors are on display, so a pristine form is not validated just because the language changed. Deliberately generic: the toolkit stays i18n-agnostic and the same hook covers any other reason to re-validate.

## Changed
- Documented that a schema whose messages are thunks (`error: () => t('…')`) is resolved just as late as a getter schema, and is the safer of the two: `toValue(schema)` runs inside `validate()` and nowhere else, while a getter that is accidentally invoked at the call site type-checks and silently freezes the language.

# 2.1.4

## Fixed
- `createIdentifier` (in `useStructureDataManagement`/`useStructureRestApi`) now honours a custom **single** identifier passed as its second argument. Previously the custom value was ignored for non-array identifiers and the default identifier was used instead, so `createIdentifier(item, 'slug')` returned `item.id` rather than `item.slug`. The array form was unaffected.

## Added
- Added mutation testing with [Stryker](https://stryker-mutator.io/): `stryker.conf.json` and a `test:mutation` npm script. See the new [Testing](/guide/testing) guide page for what mutation testing is and how to read the results.
- Expanded the test suite with the gaps mutation testing surfaced: effect-stability tests (reactive re-evaluation of `selectedRecord`/`itemList`/pagination computeds and the ref-counted `loading` flag), effect-scope auto-teardown, direct unit tests for `stableNormalize` and `generateFallbackValue`, identifier-resolution/batch-edit/guard branches of `useStructureDataManagement`, and notification auto-hide timeouts.

# 2.1

## Added
- Added `useStructureSearchApi`, a composable built on top of `useStructureRestApi` that owns everything filter/search-related: `fetchSearch`, `watchSearch` (and `watchCurrentSearch`), `checkSearch`, `searchGet`, `searchCleanup`, `resetSearches`, `isPageCached`, `isPaginateCached`, and a search-scoped `pageItemList`.
- Added `watchTarget` to `useStructureRestApi`: watches an id source (Ref/ComputedRef/getter) and re-runs `fetchTarget` whenever it changes, with `onSuccess`/`onError`/`onSettled` callbacks.
- Exported `stableNormalize` from `src/utils/stableNormalize.ts` as part of the public API (previously an internal helper of the REST composable).

## Changed
- Search/filter concerns moved out of `useStructureRestApi` entirely: `fetchSearch`, `searchGet`, `searchGetTotal`, `searchSetTotal`, `searchCleanup`, `searchCached`, `searchTotals`, and `stableNormalize` are no longer part of `useStructureRestApi` — use `useStructureSearchApi` instead.
- `fetchPaginate` on `useStructureRestApi` is now a generic, filter-agnostic paginated fetch keyed strictly by `(lastUpdateKey, pageSize, page)`. It no longer accepts `filters`, no longer supports the `[items, total]` tuple response shape, and no longer manages a total — a search/pagination layer built on top (like `useStructureSearchApi`) derives its own key from filters and reads any server-reported total out of its own response.
- `resetAll`/`destroy` on `useStructureRestApi` no longer reset search state (there is none left to reset). Consumers using `useStructureSearchApi` get merged `resetAll`/`destroy` that also clear its `searchCached`.

## Removed
- Removed `fetchSearch`, `SearchApiResult`, `ISearchCache`, `searchGet`, `searchGetTotal`, `searchSetTotal`, and `searchCleanup` from `useStructureRestApi` (superseded by `useStructureSearchApi`).

# 2.0.1

## Added
- Added `setInitialData` to `useStructureFormValidation`, to replace the reset baseline that `resetForm`/`isDirty` use.
- Added `activateAutoHydrate` to `useStructureFormValidation`: watches a reactive source (e.g. a fetched record) and, once it resolves to a defined value, adopts it as the new reset baseline and applies it to the form.
- Exposed `showFormErrors` from `useStructureFormValidation`.

# 2.0

## Added
- Added a richer public API around `useStructureRestApi`, including:
  - `fetchPaginate`
  - pre-flight cache/freshness helpers: `checkTarget`, `checkAll`, `checkByParent`, `checkAny`, `checkSearch`, `checkPaginate`, `checkMultiple`
  - search total helpers: `searchGetTotal`, `searchSetTotal`
  - lifecycle helpers: `destroy`, `resetSearches`, `resetAll`
  - exposed `queryClient`
  - exposed `maxRecords`
- Added `generateFallbackValue()` export.
- Added `lastInsertedIdentifier`, `lastInsertedIdentifiers`, and `lastInsertedRecord` to data-management/rest composables.
- Added complete documentation site pages for composables and stores.
- Added CI, docs deployment, commitlint, and husky-based repository automation.

## Changed
- `useStructureRestApi` was substantially reworked.
- TanStack Query is now the core cache/freshness engine behind the REST composable, replacing the older internal TTL bookkeeping approach.
- Caching behavior is now more robust and explicit:
  - per-query freshness is handled through a query client
  - search/page caches are better namespaced
  - per-item cache seeding is shared across list/search/detail/mutation flows
- `fetchSearch` now supports both:
  - plain item arrays
  - tuple responses in the form `[items, total]`
- `searchGet` now takes `pageSize` into account, making search caches page-size-aware.
- `fetchPaginate` is now the dedicated pagination-oriented API instead of relying on custom `fetchAll` patterns.
- Root exports now use `export *`, making more types/helpers part of the public surface.
- Notifications and identifier generation now use a safer fallback id generator rather than assuming `crypto.randomUUID()` is always available.
- Loading management is now ref-counted, making concurrent requests safer.
- Tooling/scripts were modernized:
  - package version bumped to `2.0`
  - prettier changed to check mode by default
  - new `complete:check` script
  - husky setup moved to `prepare`
- Documentation and README were rewritten to reflect the actual library direction and usage.

## Improved
- Optimistic update/delete/create flows are more reliable, including rollback behavior.
- Cache invalidation and stale handling are more coherent across list/detail/search flows.
- Search key generation is now stable even for nested objects.
- Parent/child relation handling and record lifecycle behavior are more explicit.
- Test coverage was heavily expanded and reorganized around:
  - unit behavior
  - TTL/freshness behavior
  - pagination
  - search
  - lifecycle
  - modifier semantics
  - end-to-end intent scenarios

## Notes
- This release is not just an internal refactor: the public API evolved slightly, especially around pagination, search totals, cache inspection, and lifecycle control.
- The biggest architectural shift is that TanStack became the backbone of fetch freshness, deduplication, and cache coordination, avoiding more custom wheel-reinventing in the data layer.
